Template: carlos-emr/server-name
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: Host name clinicians will use to reach CARLOS EMR:
 Enter the fully qualified host name this server will be reached at, for
 example emr.myclinic.ca.
 .
 It becomes the nginx server_name and the subject of the TLS certificate. If
 you intend to use a Let's Encrypt certificate, this name must resolve in
 public DNS to this machine before you run "carlos-ctl cert acme".
 .
 You can change it later in /etc/carlos-emr/carlos-emr.env followed by
 "carlos-ctl init-config".

Template: carlos-emr/bind-ip
Type: string
Default: 0.0.0.0
Description: Address the CARLOS EMR front door should listen on:
 0.0.0.0 serves every network interface, which is what a clinic server
 normally needs.
 .
 Use 127.0.0.1 to keep the EMR reachable only from this machine — appropriate
 while you are staging a migration, or when a separate reverse proxy in front
 of this host terminates client connections.
 .
 The application server and the database always listen on the loopback
 interface only, whatever you choose here.

Template: carlos-emr/province
Type: select
Choices: on, bc
Default: on
Description: Billing province:
 CARLOS implements provincial billing for Ontario (on) and British Columbia
 (bc). This choice selects which database migrations are applied and which
 billing behaviour the application uses.
 .
 It CANNOT be changed afterwards: the two provinces create different tables,
 and switching means starting again from an empty database.

Template: carlos-emr/tls-mode
Type: select
Choices: selfsigned, acme, manual
Default: selfsigned
Description: TLS certificate for the front door:
 selfsigned generates a certificate now, so the EMR is served over HTTPS from
 the first minute. Browsers will warn until you replace it. This is the safe
 default because it never leaves a deployment sitting on plain HTTP.
 .
 acme obtains a free Let's Encrypt certificate with certbot. It requires that
 the host name above already resolves to this machine in public DNS and that
 port 80 is reachable from the internet.
 .
 manual leaves the certificate to you: place the chain and key at
 /etc/carlos-emr/tls/fullchain.pem and privkey.pem and run
 "carlos-ctl cert manual". Until you do, the front door serves a self-signed
 placeholder certificate (generated so nginx can start), which browsers will
 warn about.

Template: carlos-emr/acme-email
Type: string
Default:
Description: Email address for Let's Encrypt registration:
 Let's Encrypt uses this address to warn you if a certificate is about to
 expire and has not renewed. It is not published.
 .
 Leave it empty to skip requesting a certificate now; you can run
 "carlos-ctl cert acme <email>" at any time.

Template: carlos-emr/java-heap
Type: string
Default: 2g
Description: Java heap size for the CARLOS application server:
 CARLOS is a large application and will not run well below 2g. On a dedicated
 server give it roughly half of physical memory, leaving room for MariaDB's
 buffer pool and about 1 GB for the operating system.
 .
 Use the JVM's own notation, for example 4g or 6144m.

Template: carlos-emr/reset-seed-admin
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Replace the seeded administrator password with a random one?
 The database migrations seed one administrator account, "carlosdoc", with a
 password hash that is published in the CARLOS source repository — it is the
 credential the project's own test suite uses. Every installation created from
 those migrations therefore starts with the same publicly-known password and
 PIN.
 .
 Accepting this replaces both with random values and writes them to
 /etc/carlos-emr/initial-admin.txt, readable only by root.
 .
 Decline ONLY on a disposable development machine that will never hold real
 patient information.

Template: carlos-emr/install-demo-data
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Load the FICTITIOUS demonstration dataset into the new database?
 This fills an EMPTY CARLOS database with a fictitious practice for
 evaluation, training and development: about 3000 fake patients (every name
 carries a FAKE- prefix), demonstration providers, appointments, clinical
 notes, labs, prescriptions, and 60 clearly-fake referral specialists.
 .
 The load refuses to run against a database that already contains patients,
 and it only adds to the reference data the schema migrations install — with
 one exception: on a British Columbia install it REPLACES the seeded
 provincial specialist directory with the fake demonstration list.
 .
 A system holding this dataset contains publicly-known demonstration content
 and known development credentials, and must NEVER hold real patient
 information. There is no supported way to remove the demonstration data
 short of destroying the database (carlos-ctl destroy-data). Decline on any
 machine that will ever be a production system.

Template: carlos-emr/initial-credentials
Type: note
Description: CARLOS EMR is installed
 The initial administrator password and PIN have been written to
 /etc/carlos-emr/initial-admin.txt (readable only by root). Log in, change the
 password, create real accounts, disable the seeded one, and then delete that
 file.
 .
 Run "sudo carlos-ctl check" to verify the deployment, and read
 /usr/share/doc/carlos-emr/README.Debian before putting patient data on this
 system — in particular the sections on backups, which are stored on this host
 only until you configure an offsite repository.
